102:### T15 — Identity collapse: the pestle mash
103:- **Timestamps:** 75–81 s (S7) · **Clip:** clips/pestle-mash.mp4 (75–81 s)
104-- **Mechanics:** The pestle works the molcajete in repeated strokes; across them, the discrete objects (dice, cubes, houses, green flesh) progressively lose their identities and merge into a uniform green paste — real guacamole texture, flecked with the objects' colours. Stop-motion carries the collapse in visible increments: each stroke, fewer readable objects.
105-- **Why it works:** the mash is the grammar's payoff and its proof — every casting is spent at once, and the mixture's plausibility as food is the film's final claim that the procedure was real all along.
106:- **Evidence:** ev/t078-pestle-mash.jpg (pestle mid-stroke, mixture collapsing into paste)
107-
108-### T16 — Serving substitution: poker-chip tortilla chips + the dip
109-- **Timestamps:** 82–94 s (S8) · **Clip:** clips/chip-dip.mp4 (89–95 s)
110-- **Mechanics:** A serape is unrolled (the film's second location-scale change, done as a table dressing), the bowl is plated, and poker chips are scattered around it as tortilla chips — cast by shape, stacking behaviour, and table context. A hand dips one chip into the guacamole; the chip breaks under the dip, exactly as a chip does, and the payoff lands.
111-- **Why it works:** the substitution grammar survives the move from preparation to eating — the viewer is finally cast as a participant, and the break is the one physical failure the film allows, which certifies all the successes before it.
